            <li>Estimate leads, customers and ROI for a given budget with the <a href="/inbound-marketing-calculator/">inbound marketing calculator</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="answer">How Much Does Inbound Marketing Cost?</h2>
        <p>Inbound marketing costs roughly $5,000 to $20,000 a month for a full-service US agency retainer, according to Protocol 80, and about £4,500 to £6,500 a month for a basic UK package, according to Axon Garside. Costs rise with the number of channels, the volume of content and whether the work is done by an agency, freelancers or an in-house team. The figures below come from agency-published pricing, so they describe what agencies charge rather than a neutral survey.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Typical monthly cost</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Full-service inbound agency retainer (US)</td><td>$5,000–$20,000</td><td><a href="https://www.protocol80.com/blog/inbound-marketing-pricing-how-much" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Protocol 80</a></td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Basic inbound package, deliverables only (UK)</td><td>About £4,500</td><td><a href="https://www.axongarside.com/blog/how-much-does-inbound-marketing-cost" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Axon Garside (March 2025)</a></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Basic inbound package including administration (UK)</td><td>£5,500–£6,500</td><td><a href="https://www.axongarside.com/blog/how-much-does-inbound-marketing-cost" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Axon Garside (March 2025)</a></td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">Protocol 80's page carries a 2024 copyright and was first published in 2016; Axon Garside's figures are in pounds. Treat both as indicative, not fixed quotes.</p>

        <h2 id="components">Inbound Marketing Cost by Component</h2>
        <p>The cost of inbound marketing is the sum of its components: content, SEO, paid media, social, lead nurturing and software. Content production is the largest single line in Protocol 80's breakdown at about $5,000 a month, followed by multimedia at about $3,000.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Component (US)</th><th scope="col">Monthly cost</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Content production</td><td>About $5,000</td><td>Blogs, guides and gated assets</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Multimedia production</td><td>About $3,000</td><td>Video and graphics</td></tr>
              <tr><td>SEO</td><td>At least $750</td><td>Agency-managed</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>PPC management</td><td>At least $1,000</td><td>Plus at least $500 in separate ad budget</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Social media</td><td>$500–$4,000</td><td>Professional managers typically $2,000–$4,000</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Lead generation, nurturing and sales enablement</td><td>$2,000–$4,000</td><td>Email workflows and handoff to sales</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">Source: <a href="https://www.protocol80.com/blog/inbound-marketing-pricing-how-much" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Protocol 80</a>. Strategy plans are priced separately: about $5,000 for a short-term plan and upward of $20,000 for a long-term one.</p>

        <h2 id="uk">Inbound Marketing Retainers by Channel (UK Averages)</h2>
        <p>UK agency retainers average between £587.50 and £1,580.85 a month per channel, according to AdZooma figures cited by Axon Garside. Paid social management is the cheapest line and email marketing the most expensive, and content is priced per piece at about £325 a post.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Channel (UK, GBP)</th><th scope="col">Average monthly retainer</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>SEO</td><td>£901.50</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Organic social media</td><td>£1,120</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Paid social management</td><td>£587.50</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Content marketing</td><td>£817.90</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Search and PPC management</td><td>£1,040.90</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Email marketing</td><td>£1,580.85</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">Source: AdZooma averages as cited in <a href="https://www.axongarside.com/blog/how-much-does-inbound-marketing-cost" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Axon Garside (March 2025)</a>. Agencies typically charge about 15% of total advertising budget as a management fee on ad spend.</p>

        <h2 id="software">Inbound Marketing Software Costs</h2>
        <p>Inbound software adds a platform fee on top of agency or staff costs. HubSpot's published Marketing Hub pricing starts at $800 a month for Professional and $3,600 a month for Enterprise, each with a one-time onboarding fee of $3,000 and $7,000 respectively. A free tier is available for basic forms and email.</p>
        <p>Lower-cost email and CRM tools can replace a full platform for smaller programmes. The right choice depends on how many contacts and workflows the programme runs; see the <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-roi/">marketing automation ROI guide</a> for a payback check before committing to a tier.</p>

        <h2 id="budgets">Three Inbound Marketing Budgets Compared</h2>
        <p>Adding the cited component figures gives three illustrative monthly budgets, from a lean programme at about $2,750 to a full programme at about $18,250. The totals are sums of the figures above, not quotes, and exclude software and ad spend beyond the stated minimum.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Budget</th><th scope="col">What it covers</th><th scope="col">Illustrative monthly total</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Lean</td><td>SEO ($750), social ($500), PPC management ($1,000) and minimum ad budget ($500)</td><td><strong>$2,750</strong></td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Core</td><td>Content ($5,000), SEO ($750), PPC management ($1,000) and lead nurturing at the low end ($2,000)</td><td><strong>$8,750</strong></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Full</td><td>All components: content, multimedia, SEO, PPC and ad budget, social and nurturing at the high end</td><td><strong>$18,250</strong></td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The Core budget lands inside Protocol 80's $5,000 to $20,000 agency range, and the Full budget sits near the top of it.</p>

        <h2 id="agency-vs-inhouse">Agency, Freelancer or In-House?</h2>
        <p>An agency retainer bundles strategy, content and reporting into one monthly fee, while freelancers and in-house hires split those costs across several contracts. Gartner's 2026 CMO Spend Survey found labour averages 24.5% of marketing budgets, up from 21.9% the year before, so staffing is already the largest controllable cost for many teams.</p>
        <p>Agencies suit teams that need several skills at once and cannot yet justify full-time hires. In-house suits programmes with steady volume and enough work to keep specialists busy. Freelancers fit a single channel such as SEO or content, where scope is clear.</p>

        <h2 id="budget-check">How to Check Whether an Inbound Budget Is Reasonable</h2>
        <p>An inbound budget is reasonable when the cost per lead and customer implied by the plan stays below what a customer is worth. Work backward from revenue targets rather than forward from an agency quote.</p>
        <ol class="numbered-steps">
          <li><strong>Set the revenue goal.</strong> Decide how much new revenue inbound must produce in 12 months.</li>
          <li><strong>Convert to customers and leads.</strong> Divide by average deal value, then by the lead-to-customer close rate, to get the leads needed.</li>
          <li><strong>Compare cost per lead.</strong> Divide the proposed monthly budget by expected monthly leads and check it against customer lifetime value using the <a href="/cac-calculator/">CAC calculator</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Compare with the budget norm.</strong> Check the total against the share of revenue peers spend; Gartner reports marketing budgets average 7.8% of company revenue in 2026.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The <a href="/inbound-marketing-calculator/">inbound marketing calculator</a> estimates leads, customers and ROI from a given monthly budget.</p>

